# =============================================================================
# SUBURBAN-SOC PLATFORM: EMULATION-TO-TELEMETRY MAPPING MATRIX
# Two-lane coverage: NETWORK/LINUX (Zeek) + WINDOWS ENDPOINT (Sysmon/4688).
# Each row is one coherent emulation -> detection -> telemetry triple.
# =============================================================================

# ----------------------------- NETWORK / LINUX LANE (Zeek) -----------------------------

[EMULATION: RECONNAISSANCE]
Execution_Vector  : tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_portscan.sh
Log_Source        : scripts/setup/configs/zeek/scan-detection.zeek -> notice.log
ECS_Mapping       : threat.technique.id = "T1046" | threat.tactic.name = "Discovery"
Target_Sigma_Rule : rules/sigma/net_zeek_port_scan.yml
NIST_CSF_Control  : DE.CM-01 (Security Continuous Monitoring)

# BROKEN PAIRING (#383, security-auditor, #365 review): renamed
# EXPLOITATION -> EXPLOITATION_UNVERIFIED so the gap below reaches every
# automated rendering of this file (console table, --json, --markdown),
# not just this comment - validate_emulation_map.py parses out comment
# lines entirely (never attached to the Emulation object), so a plain
# "EXPLOITATION" name would still show a bare, indistinguishable PASS in
# every consumer of this file, including the CI step literally named
# "purple-team loop is real" and SOP-147 Step 0.4's "expect 22/22 green."
# sim_malware_download.sh's default sample (EICAR over HTTPS) neither
# crosses bat0 nor exposes the file to Zeek at all; its documented
# plaintext-HTTP fallback yields mime_type=application/zip, which is in
# neither this rule's old 9-entry mime_type list nor the new 4-entry one
# #365 shipped. This section's Execution_Vector/Target_Sigma_Rule pairing
# passes validate_emulation_map.py (file existence + T1105 tag match
# only — it has no way to verify an emulation's ACTUAL telemetry output,
# see #383's own text) but does NOT produce telemetry this rule's
# detection logic matches. The RULE itself is NOT in question — it's
# verified against a real Elasticsearch with the real compiled Lucene
# query (tests/detections/test_live_fire.py's
# test_zeek_executable_download_fires_against_real_es) and #365 live-
# verified every mime_type against the pinned Zeek image. What's missing
# is only an emulation that produces a matching payload — see
# net_zeek_executable_download.yml's own description for the full
# disclosure. #383 closed by disclosing this gap rather than building a
# new working emulation (a genuinely matching payload needs its own
# design/review, out of scope for a docs fix) — left wired here (not
# removed) so the T1105/PR.IR-02 mapping intent stays visible for
# whoever picks up a real emulation for this rule.
[EMULATION: EXPLOITATION_UNVERIFIED]
Execution_Vector  : tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_malware_download.sh
Log_Source        : configs/network/filebeat.yml -> files.log
ECS_Mapping       : threat.technique.id = "T1105" | threat.tactic.name = "Command and Control"
Target_Sigma_Rule : rules/sigma/net_zeek_executable_download.yml
NIST_CSF_Control  : PR.IR-02 (Infrastructure Resilience)

[EMULATION: CREDENTIAL_ACCESS_SSH]
Execution_Vector  : tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_brute_ssh.sh
Log_Source        : configs/systemd/zeek-host-capture.service -> notice.log
ECS_Mapping       : threat.technique.id = "T1110" | threat.tactic.name = "Credential Access"
Target_Sigma_Rule : rules/sigma/net_zeek_ssh_bruteforce.yml
NIST_CSF_Control  : DE.AE-02 (Detection and Anomaly Validation)

# #332: same emulation script, second independent detection path — the
# session-cadence threshold rule reads zeek.ssh directly (raw session
# count per source), not the aggregated notice.log the entry above reads,
# so both legitimately fire from one sim_brute_ssh.sh run without a new
# emulation script.
# ZERO-MARGIN CAVEAT (security-auditor finding): sim_brute_ssh.sh's default
# BRUTE_PASSWORDS issues exactly 5 attempts, exactly meeting this rule's
# threshold.value:5 — any single dropped/timed-out/throttled connection
# (ConnectTimeout expiring, sshd MaxStartups, a missing SSH::Info$client
# banner on a lossy vantage) makes the emulation run silently fail to
# trigger this rule, while validate_emulation_map.py still reports this
# mapping green (it checks file existence/tag/platform sanity only, not
# threshold margin). Not fixed here — bumping the shared script's attempt
# count affects net_zeek_ssh_bruteforce.yml's own emulation pairing too.
[EMULATION: CREDENTIAL_ACCESS_SSH_CADENCE]
Execution_Vector  : tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_brute_ssh.sh
Log_Source        : configs/systemd/zeek-host-capture.service -> ssh.log
ECS_Mapping       : threat.technique.id = "T1110" | threat.tactic.name = "Credential Access"
Target_Sigma_Rule : rules/sigma/net_zeek_ssh_session_cadence.yml
NIST_CSF_Control  : DE.AE-02 (Detection and Anomaly Validation)

# #392: same emulation script and dataset as CREDENTIAL_ACCESS_SSH_CADENCE
# above, a third independent detection path over the identical zeek.ssh
# session-count signal, just a higher per-source threshold (15, not 5) over
# a longer lookback (5m run interval / 35m lookback, corrected per the
# security-auditor HIGH finding on the paired threshold rule itself - see
# that rule's own description).
# INSUFFICIENT-BY-DEFAULT CAVEAT (worse than the zero-margin caveat above,
# not just tight): sim_brute_ssh.sh's default BRUTE_PASSWORDS issues exactly
# 5 attempts - one third of this rule's threshold.value:15. The default run
# does NOT exercise this rule at all, silently, the same way
# validate_emulation_map.py reports this mapping green regardless (file
# existence/tag/platform sanity only). Exercising this rule for real needs
# an explicit override WITH MARGIN (security-auditor: 15 words would
# reintroduce the same zero-margin defect the caveat above already warns
# about) AND a real capture-path target, e.g.:
#   BRUTE_PASSWORDS="w1 w2 w3 w4 w5 w6 w7 w8 w9 w10 w11 w12 w13 w14 w15 \
#     w16 w17 w18 w19 w20" \
#   TARGET_HOST=<a host reachable over the real capture interface> \
#     tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_brute_ssh.sh
# (BRUTE_PASSWORDS is a space-separated list; the script issues one SSH
# attempt per word). TARGET_HOST defaults to 127.0.0.1
# (tests/anomaly_simulation/.env.example) - loopback traffic is invisible
# to every real capture path in this repo (CAPTURE_IFACE, not lo), so the
# documented override alone produces ZERO zeek.ssh documents on a default
# config, the same defect class as the EXPLOITATION_UNVERIFIED disclosure
# above. Also note: this produces 1 guess per session (NumberOfPasswordPrompts=1
# in sim_brute_ssh.sh), not the 6-per-session shape the rule's own threat
# model discusses - a volume/session-count smoke test, not a rate- or
# guess-count-accurate emulation. Not fixed by changing the script's own
# default - doing so would also change CREDENTIAL_ACCESS_SSH/CREDENTIAL_
# ACCESS_SSH_CADENCE's own emulation volume above.
[EMULATION: CREDENTIAL_ACCESS_SSH_CADENCE_SUSTAINED]
Execution_Vector  : tests/anomaly_simulation/sim_brute_ssh.sh
Log_Source        : configs/systemd/zeek-host-capture.service -> ssh.log
ECS_Mapping       : threat.technique.id = "T1110" | threat.tactic.name = "Credential Access"
Target_Sigma_Rule : rules/sigma/net_zeek_ssh_session_cadence_sustained.yml
NIST_CSF_Control  : DE.AE-02 (Detection and Anomaly Validation)

